+**Formulas:**
+- Position normalization for Morton encoding: $pos = ((input\_pos - boundsMin) \times (1.0f / (boundsMax - boundsMin))) \times kScaler$, where $kScaler = (1 \ll 21) - 1$ [S1, unofficial].
+- Morton code generation: $code = MortonEncode3(ipos)$, where $ipos$ is the normalized position cast to $uint3$ [S1, unofficial].
+- Float3 to Norm16 encoding: $enc = (v.x \times 65535.5f) \mid (v.y \times 65535.5f \ll 16) \mid (v.z \times 65535.5f \ll 32)$, stored as 4 bytes uint + 2 bytes ushort [S1, unofficial].
+- Float3 to Norm11 encoding: $enc = (v.x \times 2047.5f) \mid (v.y \times 1023.5f \ll 11) \mid (v.z \times 2047.5f \ll 21)$, stored as 4 bytes uint [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- Empty input arrays throw `ArgumentException` [S1, unofficial].
+- Chunk bounds must be explicitly clamped to `splatData.Length` to prevent out-of-bounds memory access [S1, unofficial].
+- Quantization reduces precision; Norm11 and Norm16 formats trade accuracy for memory savings [S1, unofficial].
